udget out of feasible range\\\")\\n    allocations = minima[:]\\n    remaining = budget - sum_min\\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_ratio = -1.0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if allocations[i] < capacities[i]:\\n                ratio = importances[i] / (allocations[i] + 1.0)\\n                if ratio > best_ratio:\\n                    best_ratio = ratio\\n                    best_idx = i\\n        if best_idx == -1:\\n            break\\n        allocations[best_idx] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    by_name = {names[i]: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}\\n    total = sum(allocations)\\n    return {'allocations': allocations, 'by_name': by_name, 'total': total}\"}\n\nWe need to ensure all quotes inside are escaped. In the code, there are single quotes, which are fine inside double-quoted JSON string. But we have double quotes inside the code (e.g., \"Missing or invalid 'segments'\" has double quotes around the string). Those need to be escaped as \\\". Also f-strings with double quotes? Actually we used f\\\"Segment {i} missing fields\\\" which has double quotes around the string. So we need to escape those. Use \\\" for each. Also the final return uses single quotes, no issue.\n\nLet's fix: In the code string, every double quote inside must be escaped as \\\". So we replace \" with \\\" in the code. For example: \"Missing or invalid 'segments'\" becomes \\\"Missing or invalid 'segments'\\\". Also \"Budget must be integer\" -> \\\"Budget must be integer\\\". etc.\n\nAdditionally, we have f-strings with quotes: f\\\"Segment {i} missing fields\\\" -> f\\\"Segment {i} missing fields\\\". That's fine. Also \"Importance must be numeric\" etc.\n\nWe also have \"Budget out of feasible range\". All need escaping.\n\nAlso note: the code uses backslashes for line breaks? No, we use literal \\n to represent newlines. That's fine. The JSON string will have \\n escaped as \\\\n? Actually in JSON string, newline is represented as \\n (two characters: backslash and n). That's correct. So our implementation string will contain \\n for line breaks. But we are writing the string literal in JSON text, so we need to write the backslash as \\\\? Wait: When we construct the JSON text ourselves, we need to produce a string that when parsed by JSON parser, yields the Python source code with actual newline characters. The JSON specification says that within a string, the newline character is represented as \\n. So we write literally the two characters backslash and n. In our response text, we will have \\n as two characters. That's fine. But we also need to escape backslashes in the code? The code might have backslashes? Not here. So no issue.\n\nBut we have to be careful about escaping double quotes. In the JSON string, we must write \\\" for each double quote. So we will replace all double quotes in the code with \\\". We'll do that.\n\nLet's write the code with escaped quotes.\n\nI'll produce the final JSON. Also ensure no trailing whitespace issues.\n\nWe'll also note: the output schema expects \"allocations\" array of integers, \"by_name\" object with integer values, \"total\" integer. Our code returns those.\n\nNow produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1496,"completion_tokens":3436,"total_tokens":4932,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2907},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":88},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}